None of the surveyed works simultaneously tackle (1) spectral over-smoothing, (2) privacy leakage from node embeddings, and (3) individual-/group-fairness of the learned representations. Existing anti–over-smoothing techniques (LASER-GNN, NDLS, ElasticGNN, PairNorm, DGN, etc.) focus solely on representation decay; they neither control mutual information w.r.t. sensitive attributes nor provide differential-privacy guarantees. Conversely, fairness-oriented or privacy-oriented graph methods (e.g.and FairDrop, DP-GCN) do not analyse or mitigate deep-layer over-smoothing. FAIR-LASER introduces several ideas that do not appear in the listed literature: 
• a Bayes-risk-minimising sparse Lanczos–Krylov basis that replaces heuristic Chebyshev/Jacobi atoms; 
• tri-objective, per-node per-layer spectral gates learned on the Pareto frontier of utility, privacy leakage (Rényi MI) and group fairness—no prior work couples these three losses in the frequency domain; 
• frequency-selective DP noise injection that adds perturbation only on bands whose gates are open, with a formal (ε,δ) proof; 
• an analytic class-margin decay bound (𝔼[margin_L] ≥ (1−λ)^L) that extends beyond variance preservation proofs seen in LASER-GNN; 
• tokenised spectral explanations via VQ-VAE, offering auditability of frequency attention per social group. 
This combination, especially the spectral privacy/fairness mechanisms and the new bound, is absent from the related corpus, giving the approach a clear element of originality.
